<LessonShell chapterNumber={2} lessonNumber={2}>

## Text Is a Thing You Can Hold

`print()` puts text on the screen. That much you saw last lesson. This lesson is about the text itself — because before Python can print something, you have to be able to *write it down*, and there are rules.

A piece of text in Python is called a **string**, and you write one by wrapping it in quotes:

```python
print("Karel the Robot")
```

The quotes are not part of the text. They are the fence that tells Python where the text starts and stops. Without them, Python would try to read `Karel` as the name of something — and there is nothing called `Karel`, so the program would stop with an error.

That distinction matters more than it looks:

- `print("beepers")` prints the word **beepers**
- `print(beepers)` looks for something *named* `beepers` and prints its value

You have already seen the second form. Last lesson's `print("Step number", i)` printed the word "Step number" and then whatever number `i` was holding. Quotes are the difference between a word and a name.

## Single or Double, and Why You Care

Python accepts both `'single quotes'` and `"double quotes"`, and they mean exactly the same thing. Having two is not indecision — it is an escape hatch.

The problem: a string ends at the first matching quote Python finds. So `'Karel's bag'` ends after `Karel`, and the rest is gibberish Python cannot parse. The fix is to fence the string with the *other* kind of quote.

<PythonEnvironment config={quotesConfig} />

The rule of thumb: **if your text contains an apostrophe, wrap it in double quotes. If it contains double quotes, wrap it in single quotes.** Some people always use double quotes and reach for singles only when they must; that is a fine habit and the one this curriculum follows.

> **If you need both kinds inside one string**, there is a third trick called an escape character. It can wait — Chapter 5 is a whole chapter on strings and it belongs there.

---

## Commas and Plus Signs Do Different Jobs

There are two ways to build a line out of pieces, and beginners mix them up constantly.

**Commas** hand `print` several separate values, and `print` puts a space between each one as it prints them.

**A plus sign** glues two strings into one longer string *before* `print` ever sees it. It adds nothing between them — not even a space. If you want a space, you have to put it there yourself.

<PythonEnvironment config={commaVsPlusConfig} />

Run that and look at the second line. `"Karel" + "found"` is `Karelfound`, because `+` glued them exactly as written. The third line fixes it by putting spaces inside the strings, where they show up as part of the text.

So which should you use? **Commas, most of the time** — they are shorter and the spacing is usually what you want. Reach for `+` when you need a piece of text pressed right up against another with no gap, like punctuation:

```python
print("Hello, " + name + "!")
```

With commas that would come out as `Hello,  name !` with stray spaces around the exclamation mark. This is the one job `+` does that commas cannot.

> **`+` joins text to text only.** Gluing a string to a number is an error, and a famously confusing one. Lesson 4 explains what is going on and how to get around it.

---

## Multiplying a String

This looks like a typo and is not:

```python
print("=" * 24)
```

Multiplying a string by a number repeats it. `"=" * 24` is a string of twenty-four equals signs, and you did not have to count them out. It is the tidiest way to draw a divider.

<PythonEnvironment config={bannerConfig} />

Notice `print()` with empty parentheses on its own — that prints a blank line, which is how you get breathing room between blocks of output.

> **Why does multiplication work on text at all?** Multiplying is supposed to be a numbers thing, and `+` joining two strings is odd for the same reason. You are seeing a real Python feature early, with the explanation deliberately held back: operators can be taught new tricks, and **Chapter 9** is where you learn how that works — and, if you like, teach `+` some tricks of your own. For now: it works, use it.

## Comments Came With You

`#` still means what it meant with Karel: everything after it on that line is for humans, and Python skips it. Every good habit you built writing Karel comments transfers directly.

## When It Goes Wrong

Two mistakes you will make this week, and what they look like:

- **A missing quote** — Python reaches the end of the line still looking for the closing fence and reports a `SyntaxError`. It usually points at the right line.
- **A missing parenthesis** — same idea, also a `SyntaxError`, and it sometimes blames the line *after* the real problem. If a syntax error makes no sense, check the line above it.

Errors print into the Output panel with a **traceback**. They are not a scolding — they are the most specific help you are going to get. Read the last line first; that is the one that names the problem.

